1
00:00:00,522 --> 00:00:01,521
Sunday night.

2
00:00:01,521 --> 00:00:03,540
Forty-eight answer sheets from this morning's mock

3
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:04,368
test.

4
00:00:04,368 --> 00:00:05,539
One red pen.

5
00:00:05,539 --> 00:00:07,809
And a cup of chai that went cold an hour ago.

6
00:00:07,939 --> 00:00:09,272
You know how this goes.

7
00:00:09,272 --> 00:00:10,655
Tick, cross, tick…

8
00:00:10,655 --> 00:00:11,362
total.

9
00:00:11,362 --> 00:00:13,724
Every mark into the register, then the rank list.

10
00:00:13,724 --> 00:00:15,657
And somewhere around Tuesday, it's finally done.

11
00:00:15,788 --> 00:00:18,261
But the one question that really matters —

12
00:00:18,261 --> 00:00:20,330
which questions did my batch get wrong,

13
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:20,962
and why?

14
00:00:20,962 --> 00:00:22,290
never gets answered.

15
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,430
To know that, you'd have to count nine hundred and

16
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:26,065
sixty answers by hand.

17
00:00:26,065 --> 00:00:27,549
Nobody has that kind of Sunday.

18
00:00:27,679 --> 00:00:30,375
On TestoZa, the checking is done the second they press

19
00:00:30,375 --> 00:00:30,970
Submit.

20
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:33,454
But then you open the results, and it's a screen full

21
00:00:33,454 --> 00:00:34,413
of numbers.

22
00:00:34,413 --> 00:00:36,200
So today, let's read them together.

23
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:38,643
Both result pages, every number, and exactly what

24
00:00:38,643 --> 00:00:40,341
to do with them on Monday morning.

25
00:00:41,341 --> 00:00:43,855
First, a promise, because I know this is the worry.

26
00:00:43,855 --> 00:00:45,258
Your marks register?

27
00:00:45,258 --> 00:00:46,671
That's the Excel file.

28
00:00:46,671 --> 00:00:48,539
The rank list for the notice board?

29
00:00:48,539 --> 00:00:50,821
One tap, and it's on WhatsApp.

30
00:00:50,821 --> 00:00:53,223
Opening one student's copy to see where they went

31
00:00:53,223 --> 00:00:53,769
wrong?

32
00:00:53,769 --> 00:00:55,536
You can open every single answer sheet.

33
00:00:55,536 --> 00:00:57,151
And your discussion class stays.

34
00:00:57,151 --> 00:00:59,065
It just gets shorter, and a lot sharper.

35
00:00:59,196 --> 00:01:01,649
And then there are a few things paper simply can't

36
00:01:01,649 --> 00:01:02,618
tell you.

37
00:01:02,618 --> 00:01:03,533
Let's go find them.

38
00:01:04,646 --> 00:01:06,403
Your results live in two places.

39
00:01:06,403 --> 00:01:09,280
On My Tests, every exam you've run has a Results button.

40
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:12,309
And in the sidebar, All Submissions keeps every exam

41
00:01:12,309 --> 00:01:14,186
your candidates have ever written.

42
00:01:14,186 --> 00:01:15,665
Let's start with the quick view.

43
00:01:15,752 --> 00:01:17,257
Click Results.

44
00:01:17,257 --> 00:01:20,548
Up top: the exam, its status — this one has ended

45
00:01:20,548 --> 00:01:23,356
twenty questions, forty marks, twenty minutes.

46
00:01:23,443 --> 00:01:26,259
These four boxes are your attendance register.

47
00:01:26,259 --> 00:01:27,864
Fifty-two candidates started.

48
00:01:27,864 --> 00:01:29,803
Writing now shows who's still writing,

49
00:01:29,803 --> 00:01:31,388
live, during the exam.

50
00:01:31,388 --> 00:01:33,367
It's over, so, zero.

51
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:34,800
Forty-eight submitted.

52
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,425
And four dropped: they started, but never handed in.

53
00:01:37,512 --> 00:01:40,390
Tap Dropped, and you can see exactly when each one

54
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:42,630
left, and how much they'd answered.

55
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:45,326
Akash answered two questions and went quiet at three

56
00:01:45,326 --> 00:01:46,447
minutes past ten.

57
00:01:46,447 --> 00:01:48,153
Sonal answered fourteen.

58
00:01:48,153 --> 00:01:49,425
That's a different story.

59
00:01:49,425 --> 00:01:51,333
One quick call tells you if it was the network,

60
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:52,909
the phone, or something else.

61
00:01:52,909 --> 00:01:55,301
On paper, both of them were just…

62
00:01:55,301 --> 00:01:55,900
absent.

63
00:01:55,987 --> 00:01:57,996
Now Submitted, everyone who finished.

64
00:01:57,996 --> 00:02:00,550
Average, highest and lowest, right at the top.

65
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:02,761
Then every score, with the percentage under it:

66
00:02:02,761 --> 00:02:05,477
blue means forty percent or more, red means below.

67
00:02:05,477 --> 00:02:07,092
A tick for right, a cross for wrong,

68
00:02:07,092 --> 00:02:08,485
a dash for left blank.

69
00:02:08,485 --> 00:02:10,440
Time taken, and when they submitted.

70
00:02:10,527 --> 00:02:11,385
Want ranks?

71
00:02:11,385 --> 00:02:12,546
Switch on Rank.

72
00:02:12,546 --> 00:02:15,191
Hover the little i for the tie-breakers.

73
00:02:15,191 --> 00:02:17,331
Same as the real exam: higher marks first,

74
00:02:17,331 --> 00:02:19,199
then fewer wrong answers, then fewer left blank,

75
00:02:19,199 --> 00:02:20,844
then whoever submitted first.

76
00:02:20,844 --> 00:02:22,843
Look at Sneha and Rahul: both thirty-three,

77
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:24,903
both two wrong, both one blank.

78
00:02:24,903 --> 00:02:26,902
Sneha submitted four minutes earlier,

79
00:02:26,902 --> 00:02:28,103
so Sneha is second.

80
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:30,219
This Submitted heading is a filter too.

81
00:02:30,219 --> 00:02:32,107
If one test was written on two days,

82
00:02:32,107 --> 00:02:34,085
pick a day, and the ranks follow.

83
00:02:34,085 --> 00:02:36,327
Excel downloads the whole list: names,

84
00:02:36,327 --> 00:02:38,114
roll numbers, marks, ranks.

85
00:02:38,114 --> 00:02:38,910
That's your register.

86
00:02:38,910 --> 00:02:39,527
Done.

87
00:02:39,527 --> 00:02:41,043
And search finds anyone in a second.

88
00:02:41,379 --> 00:02:44,176
Now click this, on any row, and you get that candidate's

89
00:02:44,176 --> 00:02:45,559
whole answer sheet.

90
00:02:45,559 --> 00:02:47,608
Jyoti: eighteen out of forty.

91
00:02:47,608 --> 00:02:48,910
Not great, on paper.

92
00:02:48,910 --> 00:02:50,485
But look at it subject by subject.

93
00:02:50,485 --> 00:02:52,383
English: ten out of ten.

94
00:02:52,383 --> 00:02:53,363
Full marks.

95
00:02:53,363 --> 00:02:54,554
And Quant…

96
00:02:54,554 --> 00:02:55,321
minus two.

97
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:56,321
She isn't a weak student.

98
00:02:56,321 --> 00:02:58,572
She's guessing in Maths, and the negative marking

99
00:02:58,572 --> 00:03:00,066
is eating her score.

100
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:02,328
Topic Performance breaks it down even further,

101
00:03:02,328 --> 00:03:05,174
and Questions breakdown shows every answer she gave,

102
00:03:05,174 --> 00:03:07,203
even the seconds she spent on each one.

103
00:03:07,203 --> 00:03:09,192
It's exactly like opening her copy.

104
00:03:09,192 --> 00:03:10,548
Except you never had to check it.

105
00:03:10,635 --> 00:03:12,643
This list tells you who scored what.

106
00:03:12,643 --> 00:03:13,876
It doesn't tell you why.

107
00:03:13,876 --> 00:03:15,469
For that: Full report.

108
00:03:16,096 --> 00:03:18,408
This is the page to open the morning after.

109
00:03:18,408 --> 00:03:20,638
Forty-eight candidates, the day it was written,

110
00:03:20,638 --> 00:03:22,144
twenty questions, forty marks.

111
00:03:22,231 --> 00:03:23,867
How the class did.

112
00:03:23,867 --> 00:03:27,562
The average: fifty-four percent, about twenty-two

113
00:03:27,562 --> 00:03:28,753
out of forty.

114
00:03:28,753 --> 00:03:30,823
Highest, Aditi, ninety-four.

115
00:03:30,823 --> 00:03:32,377
Lowest, eight.

116
00:03:32,377 --> 00:03:34,810
And eleven candidates need help: they're below forty

117
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:35,316
percent.

118
00:03:35,403 --> 00:03:37,200
Now this chart, how the marks spread.

119
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:38,057
Most people skip it.

120
00:03:38,057 --> 00:03:39,017
Please don't.

121
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:41,450
Each bar is a group of students: zero to nineteen

122
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:44,217
percent, twenty to thirty-nine, and so on.

123
00:03:44,217 --> 00:03:45,963
Red is trouble, amber is getting there,

124
00:03:45,963 --> 00:03:46,873
green is strong.

125
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:49,084
And the shape tells you something.

126
00:03:49,084 --> 00:03:51,366
If everything piles up on the right,

127
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:53,617
the paper was too easy.

128
00:03:53,617 --> 00:03:54,981
Make the next one tougher.

129
00:03:54,981 --> 00:03:56,162
A pile on the left?

130
00:03:56,162 --> 00:03:58,211
Either the paper was too hard, or the chapter didn't

131
00:03:58,211 --> 00:03:58,817
land.

132
00:03:58,817 --> 00:04:01,452
And two humps, one at each end, means your batch has

133
00:04:01,452 --> 00:04:02,896
split into two groups.

134
00:04:02,896 --> 00:04:05,329
That's when a separate doubt class for the lower group

135
00:04:05,329 --> 00:04:06,349
really pays off.

136
00:04:06,349 --> 00:04:09,450
Ours is a healthy middle, with a tail that needs attention.

137
00:04:10,302 --> 00:04:12,777
And next to it, the same story in plain words.

138
00:04:12,864 --> 00:04:15,903
Below that, the rank list, medals for the top three.

139
00:04:15,903 --> 00:04:18,508
Tap Below forty percent, and there are your eleven.

140
00:04:18,508 --> 00:04:19,982
These are the students to call this week,

141
00:04:19,982 --> 00:04:21,537
or whose parents to message.

142
00:04:21,537 --> 00:04:23,333
Search works with roll numbers too,

143
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:25,436
and any name opens the same answer sheet.

144
00:04:25,523 --> 00:04:28,117
Now, honestly, the most useful card on the whole page:

145
00:04:28,117 --> 00:04:29,541
Teach these again.

146
00:04:29,541 --> 00:04:32,176
Every question fewer than half your batch got right,

147
00:04:32,176 --> 00:04:33,216
hardest first.

148
00:04:33,216 --> 00:04:35,588
So Monday's discussion class doesn't start from question

149
00:04:35,588 --> 00:04:36,234
one.

150
00:04:36,234 --> 00:04:37,647
It starts here.

151
00:04:37,647 --> 00:04:38,968
Five questions, not twenty.

152
00:04:39,055 --> 00:04:40,428
Click Q13.

153
00:04:40,428 --> 00:04:42,911
Two successive discounts, twenty and ten percent.

154
00:04:42,911 --> 00:04:44,052
The answer is twenty-eight.

155
00:04:44,052 --> 00:04:46,203
But look: fifty-four percent picked thirty.

156
00:04:46,203 --> 00:04:48,130
They simply added the two discounts.

157
00:04:48,130 --> 00:04:49,100
That's not a silly mistake.

158
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,250
That's a concept the batch has wrong,

159
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:54,289
and one example on the board fixes it for everyone.

160
00:04:54,376 --> 00:04:56,485
Now Q15, time and work.

161
00:04:56,485 --> 00:04:58,484
Only twenty-one percent got it right.

162
00:04:58,484 --> 00:05:01,241
But look at the grey: almost half left it blank.

163
00:05:01,241 --> 00:05:02,290
They didn't get it wrong.

164
00:05:02,290 --> 00:05:03,109
They never tried it.

165
00:05:03,109 --> 00:05:05,430
So either time ran out, or the chapter isn't done

166
00:05:05,430 --> 00:05:06,087
yet.

167
00:05:06,087 --> 00:05:08,621
Two completely different fixes, and on paper you'd

168
00:05:08,621 --> 00:05:09,976
never have seen the difference.

169
00:05:10,063 --> 00:05:11,729
And Q9, Loktak Lake.

170
00:05:11,729 --> 00:05:13,869
The answers are spread almost evenly across all four

171
00:05:13,869 --> 00:05:14,636
options.

172
00:05:14,636 --> 00:05:15,717
That's what guessing looks like.

173
00:05:15,717 --> 00:05:17,715
Nobody really knew it, so it goes on the revision

174
00:05:17,715 --> 00:05:19,596
list, not into a whole new lesson.

175
00:05:20,218 --> 00:05:22,964
So: one wrong option standing tall is a trap,

176
00:05:22,964 --> 00:05:24,115
reteach the concept.

177
00:05:24,115 --> 00:05:27,437
A lot of grey means time, or an unfinished chapter.

178
00:05:27,437 --> 00:05:28,840
And everything flat?

179
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:29,627
Guessing.

180
00:05:29,627 --> 00:05:30,361
Revise it.

181
00:05:31,474 --> 00:05:34,503
Flip to Hardest first to see the whole paper by difficulty.

182
00:05:34,503 --> 00:05:36,986
And By section shows your batch subject by subject.

183
00:05:36,986 --> 00:05:38,238
Reasoning, seventy-five.

184
00:05:38,238 --> 00:05:39,197
English, seventy.

185
00:05:39,197 --> 00:05:40,429
General Awareness, fifty-seven.

186
00:05:40,429 --> 00:05:41,751
And Quant, forty-four.

187
00:05:41,751 --> 00:05:43,801
So now you know exactly where next week's extra class

188
00:05:43,801 --> 00:05:44,252
goes.

189
00:05:44,339 --> 00:05:46,177
Finally, sending it out.

190
00:05:46,177 --> 00:05:48,448
Send rank list opens WhatsApp with the whole list

191
00:05:48,448 --> 00:05:51,063
already typed, and you choose who gets it.

192
00:05:51,063 --> 00:05:53,173
Copy, if you'd rather paste it somewhere else.

193
00:05:53,173 --> 00:05:55,616
Excel gives you two sheets: the rank list,

194
00:05:55,616 --> 00:05:57,978
and every question with its most-picked wrong option.

195
00:05:57,978 --> 00:06:00,635
And Print, for your file or the parents' meeting.

196
00:06:01,270 --> 00:06:03,209
One last place: All Submissions.

197
00:06:03,209 --> 00:06:05,823
Every exam your candidates wrote, newest first.

198
00:06:05,823 --> 00:06:08,479
The badge is the class average, and the coloured bar

199
00:06:08,479 --> 00:06:10,013
is strong, average and weak.

200
00:06:10,013 --> 00:06:12,073
Now read it from the bottom up: forty-six,

201
00:06:12,073 --> 00:06:13,759
forty-eight, fifty, fifty-four.

202
00:06:13,759 --> 00:06:15,525
Week by week, your batch is climbing,

203
00:06:15,525 --> 00:06:17,757
and that's something you can actually show a parent.

204
00:06:17,757 --> 00:06:19,644
Next week's mock is waiting right there,

205
00:06:19,644 --> 00:06:20,917
one tap from going live.

206
00:06:21,535 --> 00:06:23,130
So here's your five-minute routine,

207
00:06:23,130 --> 00:06:24,452
every Monday morning.

208
00:06:24,452 --> 00:06:26,673
One: look at the average and the spread.

209
00:06:26,673 --> 00:06:28,410
Two: send the rank list.

210
00:06:28,410 --> 00:06:30,772
Three: open Teach these again.

211
00:06:30,772 --> 00:06:32,458
That's your discussion class.

212
00:06:32,458 --> 00:06:36,052
Four: filter below forty percent, and call those students.

213
00:06:36,052 --> 00:06:38,122
Five: download the Excel for your register.

214
00:06:38,122 --> 00:06:38,627
Five minutes.

215
00:06:38,627 --> 00:06:39,574
Not a whole Sunday.

216
00:06:40,661 --> 00:06:42,095
Paper tests gave you marks.

217
00:06:42,095 --> 00:06:44,316
TestoZa gives you the reasons behind them.

218
00:06:44,316 --> 00:06:46,517
Try it on your next mock, and if this helped,

219
00:06:46,517 --> 00:06:48,283
you know where the subscribe button is.

220
00:06:48,283 --> 00:06:49,302
See you in the next one.
